<p>On every build start, and whenever a <code>*.ce.*</code> file changes, the plugin runs <a href="https://github.com/runem/web-component-analyzer">web-component-analyzer</a> over <code>js</code>, <code>templates</code> and <code>components</code>. The result gives you tag and attribute completion in Twig and HTML files.</p>

<h2>VS Code</h2>
<p>Point VS Code to the generated file:</p>
{% embed code_block with { lang: 'json', title: '.vscode/settings.json' } %}{% block code %}{% verbatim %}
{
  "html.customData": [
    "web/themes/custom/mytheme/.uebertool/vscode.html-custom-data.json"
  ]
}
{% endverbatim %}{% endblock %}{% endembed %}

<h2>Documenting elements</h2>
<p>Write a JSDoc comment with an <code>@element</code> tag. For Vue files, put it in a <code>&lt;docs&gt;</code> block:</p>
{% embed code_block with { lang: 'vue' } %}{% block code %}{% verbatim %}
<docs lang="ts">
/**
 * A Vue single file component as custom element.
 *
 * @element demo-hello-vue
 *
 * @attr {string} name - Who to greet.
 *
 * @slot - Appended after the greeting.
 */
</docs>
{% endverbatim %}{% endblock %}{% endembed %}

{% embed aside with { type: 'caution', title: '@element is required for TypeScript files' } %}
  {% block content %}
    <p>The analyzer only finds classes that are registered with <code>customElements.define()</code> or tagged with <code>@element</code>. The loader registers the elements at runtime, so add the tag to TS and TSX elements, or they won't show up.</p>
  {% endblock %}
{% endembed %}

